<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Willem Johannes Leyds</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1859">1859</a>–<a href="/years/1940">1940</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Willem Leyds</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Willem Johannes Leyds was a Dutch-born lawyer who became state attorney of the South African Republic in 1884 and its state secretary from 1888 to 1898, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paul-kruger">Paul Kruger</a>'s chief adviser. He built up a professional civil service, largely staffed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> officials, and was distrusted by the mining industry and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>. From 1898 he represented the republic as envoy in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, seeking support during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-boer-war">Second Boer War</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/willem-johannes-leyds.jpg" alt="Willem Johannes Leyds" width="320" height="368" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jan Veth · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Leyds-Veth.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
